title: Restructuring the CEC
new-page: true
view-if: (coalition_government = 1 and month = 11 and week = 3) or (coalition_government = 2 and month = 12 and week = 2) or (month = 12 and week = 2)
max-visits: 1
tags: event
on-arrival: {!
  Q.add_peasants = 0;
  Q.add_soldiers = 0;
  Q.add_unions = 0;
  Q.add_duma = 0;

  Q.new_peasant_seats = 0;
  Q.new_soldier_seats = 0;
  Q.new_union_seats = 0;
  Q.new_duma_seats = 0;
!}

= Restructuring the CEC

Ever since the moderate socialists walked out at the 2nd Congress of Soviets, the CEC has been operating with only 119 members (108 from the Bolsheviks and Left-SRs), though it was assumed the moderates would eventually return and take up additional seats. There is also the issue of how to incorporate the peasant soviets, which up to this point have been operating under a separate apparatus (the recent Peasants' Congress being attended by some 350 separate delegates). Some sort of restructuring of the CEC is required, though hopefully without having to sacrifice our party's lead within the body. 

[?if coalition_government = 1 : Now that we have negotiated a coalition agreement, our coalition partners are expecting that the overhaul will more fairly reflect the distribution of forces and the various interest groups of the working-class movement. Our own party and the Left-SRs stand to benefit most from incorporation of peasant and soldier soviets, while the right-wing is pushing for representation for the trade unions and city dumas. Though the dumas are bourgeois institutions at heart, the opposition argues that they nonetheless represent an important democratic institution, having been elected by new rules of universal suffrage. ?] 

[?if coalition_government = 2 : Now that we have negotiated a coalition agreement, the Left-SRs are expecting a favorable incorporation of the peasant soviets, as well as other various working-class bodies. The Left-SRs and our own party likely have the most to gain from representation for peasant and soldiers' soviets. Meanwhile moderate socialists still hold out hope that we will extend representation to the trade unions and city dumas. Though the dumas are bourgeois institutions at heart, the opposition argues that they nonetheless represent an important democratic institution, having been elected by new rules of universal suffrage. ?] 

[?if coalition_government < 1 : Though we failed to form a coalition agreement with any other parties, it's still prudent that we address the structure of the CEC. The Left-SRs and our own party likely have the most to gain from representation for peasant and soldiers' soviets. Meanwhile moderate socialists still hold out hope that we will extend representation to the trade unions and city dumas. Though the dumas are bourgeois institutions at heart, the opposition argues that they nonetheless represent an important democratic institution, having been elected by new rules of universal suffrage. ?] 

At the same time, overly expanding the CEC could have the unintended consequence of making it more wieldly and harder to arrange for regular meetings. 

- @restructure_menu

@restructure_menu
title: Let's begin negotiations...
